<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Augsburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1180 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Dillingen an der Donau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Swabian church state of the bishops of Augsburg, who were forced out of their own cathedral city and governed instead from Dillingen on the Danube.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The diocese of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-augsburg">Augsburg</a> dates from late Roman times and was led in the 10th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-of-augsburg">Saint Ulrich</a>, who defended the city at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Lechfeld</a> in 955. From the late 12th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> assembled a secular territory along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-river">Lech</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. As the city itself won independence as the Free Imperial City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, they moved their residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillingen-an-der-donau">Dillingen</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The cathedral chapter elected the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, who ruled scattered lands including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillingen-an-der-donau">Dillingen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fussen">Füssen</a> and parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a>. Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-truchsess-von-waldburg">Otto Truchsess von Waldburg</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-dillingen">University of Dillingen</a> in 1551, run by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> from 1563, which made the territory a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> stronghold. Its estates were rural, with farms, forests and small market towns.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The territory was secularised in 1802 and annexed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' secular rule; the last prince-bishop, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clemens-wenceslaus-of-saxony">Clemens Wenceslaus of Saxony</a>, was also Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. The Catholic diocese of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-augsburg">Augsburg</a> continues today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-augsburg.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Augsburg" width="320" height="288"><figcaption>Image: Roke (map), Hanno Sandvik (addition of colours) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HRR_1648_Hst_Augsburg.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1180 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dillingen an der Donau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Otto Truchsess von Waldburg, Clemens Wenceslaus of Saxony</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Augsburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1492&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-augsburg" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Augsburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4933 486 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Augsburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5253 742L5229 694M5253 742L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5253 742h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5253 742h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1492&amp;focus=prince-